  "reaction_name": "Synthesis of [Co(ttpp)(pba)2](OTf)",
  "observations": "21.01.2026\nWorkup\nRotovap (40 °C) (1:30 h)\nvacuum  for 30 min\nAdd 5 mL DCM\nmanually stirred and sonicated for 5 min (dissolved)\nSi-SH (0.196 g) \nstir (1000 rpm, 2 h) 12:45 --> 14:45 [stirring does not need to be this fast]\n12 mL plastic syringe, cotton plug (compressed), syringe filter (⌀ 25 mm, 0.45 μm, PTFE), 100 mL RBF [next time better to add another 0.2 µm filter]\nfiltered, transferred, and rinsed w/ 15 mL DCM (until pale green)\nHexane 90 mL\nmanually stirred and sonicated\nFridge: 4 days (15:30)\n\n26.01.2026\nFilter paper (90 mm, 15A), glass funnel, suction adapter —> 100 mL RBF [next time, tare the filter paper too.]\nFiltered and transferred w/ 2 x (3 mL DCM + 20 mL hexane) (orange filtrate, green residue)\nRinsed w/ hexane 4 x 4 mL\nDried w/ suction for 1 min\nVacuum \n20 mL screw vial (tare wt of vial (7.85387 g))\n→ CDCl₃ 600 μL -> ¹H NMR P (Bruker AV 400@U1.834; 25 °C, 20 Hz, zg30, NS 16, DS 2, D1 1 s)\nVacuum  o/n\n --> 0.08665 g\nICP → 11.4 mg\nFor sample preparation, an accurately weighed amount of compound, 11.4 mg, was transferred into microwave digestion vials. A minimal volume of concentrated nitric acid (HNO₃) was added, followed by hydrogen peroxide (H₂O₂) to a total digestion volume of approximately 5 mL. The resulting mixtures were subjected to microwave-assisted digestion at 200 °C for 2 hours to ensure complete decomposition of the organic matrix.\nAfter cooling to room temperature, the digestion mixture was transferred to a 10-mL volumetric flask and diluted to the mark with deionized water. The solution was kept overnight. The solution was transferred into the test tubes.\nInitial ICP-OES measurements resulted in signals exceeding the calibration range for cobalt, indicating that the analyte concentration was too high. Therefore, an additional dilution step was performed by transferring 2 mL of the solution into a new test tube and diluting to a final volume of 10 mL with deionized water (dilution factor = 5). These diluted samples were subsequently remeasured.",
